{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxxeCnhAgxhhZtkXrYnt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four gaps (N1–N4) now have tests, and each of the six mutants fails them. Nothing is committed. Only the two test files changed; there are no production or doc edits, and I found no production defect.\n\n| Item | Mutant | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| N1 | `closures-round-trip` reads the file-wide index instead of its own scope | killed: 1 error, `:ambiguous-image` |\n| N1 | `kernel-space` ignores the scope | killed: 2 failures, 3 errors |\n| N2 | injectivity guard removed | killed: 2 failures in `law-4-controls` |\n| N2 | law-4 descriptor guard removed | killed: 2 failures in `law-4-controls` |\n| N3 | `row-procedure` checks only the top level | killed: 24 failures |\n| N4 | stack and register image functions swapped | killed: 4 failures in `alpha-equivalent-segments` |\n\nEvery mutant was restored, checked with `diff -q`, and `layout.cljc` is clean under `git diff --quiet`. The mutants were run before formatting, and cljstyle reformatted `layout_test.cljc` afterwards. I re-ran both tiers after formatting but not the mutants.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **N1:** `kernel-space` now throws if a world is not scoped, so a missed site fails at once. `h/file-wide` is the only explicit way to read the global index, and the alpha-equivalence contamination check is its one user. Every world in `layout_test` is now scoped, plus the tampered-entry and non-safepoint cases in `layout-refusals`. `closures-round-trip` now runs codex's reproduction itself: it projects an alpha-renamed copy of each fixture into the global index before its cases run.\n- **N2:** the injectivity and descriptor guards now throw instead of using `is`, so a test can watch them fire without a reported failure. Both controls are real worlds:\n  - **Descriptor change:** an extra ring is held only on the register lane. The roots stay equal, so only the descriptor guard can catch it.\n  - **Duplicate correspondence:** on the stack lane, every served identity gets a twin pointing at the same stream, so two identities share one logical name.\n- **N3:** new test `free-env-nested-values`. A closure or continuation nested in a vector or map is refused in both directions, with the exact path or key and zero attaches or serves. The admitted controls (a primitive and an authentic cursor reference) cross in both directions and are read back through the collection.\n  - The continuation had to be captured inside `(fn [box] …)`. Otherwise the reference lane itself refuses to lift it, with `:free-env-mismatch`.\n- **N4:** the refusal's `:yin.k/image` is now pinned to each receiver's own hash. The case where only one of the pair is carried lowers into both native lanes with one attach and continues to `\"B\"`.\n\n**Verification (JVM, foreground):**\n- **Quick tier:** 67 tests, 4191 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- **Slow tier:** 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. This is 793 fewer than before. Those were the guard checks that now throw instead of counting as assertions (5 per case and lane, over 61 cases × 3 lanes), so the slow count no longer matches codex's earlier baseline.\n- **Kondo:** 0 errors, 0 warnings. cljstyle was applied to both files.\n\nOn every lane, including the reference lane, a primitive taken out of a guest collection can't be called (\"Cannot apply non-function\"). A primitive at the top level of the free environment calls normally. This is VM behaviour outside 6c, so I left it, and the primitive control returns the value instead of calling it.\n\nFiles are in `collab/`:\n- `1792390000000-engineer-6c-fix2.report.md`\n- `1792390000000-engineer-6c-fix2.mutants.py` (the mutant runner)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2756,"cache_read_input_tokens":165829,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input